1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,114
DIDs say who controls an identity.
Credentials say something about it: this

2
00:00:08,114 --> 00:00:17,580
address passed KYC, this account is a DAO
member, this user finished a course.

3
00:00:17,580 --> 00:00:26,369
In this video, using SDK 0.1.0, we'll
follow one credential through its whole

4
00:00:26,369 --> 00:00:29,750
life, from issuance to revocation.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,789
Three roles are involved.

6
00:00:32,789 --> 00:00:42,553
The issuer is a trusted organization that
makes a claim, like a KYC provider.

7
00:00:42,553 --> 00:00:48,829
The subject is the address the claim is
about.

8
00:00:48,829 --> 00:00:56,500
The verifier is any app that wants to
check the claim.

9
00:00:56,500 --> 00:01:06,263
The contract sits in the middle and
enforces the rules, so the verifier never

10
00:01:06,263 --> 00:01:09,750
has to trust the subject.

11
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:14,426
Here's what a credential looks like.

12
00:01:14,426 --> 00:01:25,491
The id is a 32-byte hash. subject and
issuer are addresses. The type is KYC,

13
00:01:25,491 --> 00:01:28,441
Reputation, Achievement or Custom.

14
00:01:28,441 --> 00:01:39,506
claims holds the assertions, like level
two or tier gold, and claims hash is a

15
00:01:39,506 --> 00:01:49,096
SHA-256 of the claims payload, so anyone
can check the claims weren't altered.

16
00:01:49,096 --> 00:01:57,948
Then there are timestamps: issued at, an
optional activation time for credentials

17
00:01:57,948 --> 00:02:08,275
that only become valid later, and expires
at, where zero means never. Finally, a

18
00:02:08,275 --> 00:02:09,750
revoked flag.

19
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:21,426
Claims are public. Store the result of a
check, not the evidence.

20
00:02:21,426 --> 00:02:37,612
A KYC credential should say this person is
verified at level two in the EU. It should

21
00:02:37,612 --> 00:02:42,372
never contain the passport number.

22
00:02:42,372 --> 00:02:53,798
The raw documents stay with the issuer,
under their own data protection

23
00:02:53,798 --> 00:02:54,750
obligations.

24
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:10,852
Not just anyone can issue credentials. The
contract admin, the address that ran

25
00:03:10,852 --> 00:03:19,388
initialize, keeps a list of trusted
issuers.

26
00:03:19,388 --> 00:03:31,582
Here the admin registers our issuer
account from the CLI.

27
00:03:31,582 --> 00:03:48,653
Adding the same issuer twice does nothing,
and if an unregistered address tries to

28
00:03:48,653 --> 00:03:54,750
issue, the contract rejects it.

29
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:08,897
Issuing is one call. Create a
CredentialClient, hash the claims with

30
00:04:08,897 --> 00:04:22,793
SHA-256, then call issue credential with
the issuer's keypair, the subject's

31
00:04:22,793 --> 00:04:35,427
address, the type, the claims, the hash
and an expiry.

32
00:04:35,427 --> 00:04:56,903
The expiry is a Unix timestamp in seconds,
here one year from now. Pass zero for a

33
00:04:56,903 --> 00:05:01,957
credential that never expires.

34
00:05:01,957 --> 00:05:18,380
The response has the new credential ID in
data, plus the transaction hash.

35
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:29,750
That ID is what the subject shares with
verifiers.

36
00:05:30,000 --> 00:05:49,069
The contract keeps an index of credentials
per subject, so you can list everything an

37
00:05:49,069 --> 00:05:51,612
address holds.

38
00:05:51,612 --> 00:06:08,138
The first argument is just the account
used to build the read-only simulation.

39
00:06:08,138 --> 00:06:11,952
Nothing is signed.

40
00:06:11,952 --> 00:06:28,479
There's also get credentials by issuer,
for the issuer's side of the same

41
00:06:28,479 --> 00:06:29,750
question.

42
00:06:30,000 --> 00:06:41,381
Now the verifier's side. verify credential
asks the contract whether this credential

43
00:06:41,381 --> 00:06:49,917
exists, isn't revoked, has become active,
and hasn't expired.

44
00:06:49,917 --> 00:07:04,143
It returns valid, and when valid is false,
a reason: not found, revoked, expired, or

45
00:07:04,143 --> 00:07:06,988
not yet active.

46
00:07:06,988 --> 00:07:19,317
Verification is a simulation: it's free,
needs no signature, and doesn't write to

47
00:07:19,317 --> 00:07:29,750
the ledger. For many IDs at once, use
verify credential batch.

48
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:47,537
Show the reason to users. "Invalid" is a
dead end; "expired, ask your issuer to

49
00:07:47,537 --> 00:07:55,721
renew it" tells them what to do.

50
00:07:55,721 --> 00:08:09,750
The reference app's Credentials tab shows
a different message for each reason.

51
00:08:10,000 --> 00:08:39,263
Anyone can run the same check in the app's
Credentials tab: paste an ID and verify,

52
00:08:39,263 --> 00:08:44,750
no wallet needed.

53
00:08:45,000 --> 00:09:01,412
Before recording, I issued a credential
that expired two minutes later. Verifying

54
00:09:01,412 --> 00:09:10,985
it now gives valid false, reason expired.

55
00:09:10,985 --> 00:09:27,397
Expiry is checked against the ledger's
clock, not the verifier's, so everyone

56
00:09:27,397 --> 00:09:32,868
gets the same answer.

57
00:09:32,868 --> 00:09:52,015
The original issuer can renew it by
setting a later expiry. Verify again, and

58
00:09:52,015 --> 00:09:54,750
it's valid.

59
00:09:55,000 --> 00:10:07,954
Sometimes an issuer has to take a
credential back: fraud was detected, a

60
00:10:07,954 --> 00:10:13,932
membership lapsed, a mistake was made.

61
00:10:13,932 --> 00:10:27,882
revoke credential marks it revoked. Only
the original issuer can do this, not the

62
00:10:27,882 --> 00:10:33,861
subject and not even the admin.

63
00:10:33,861 --> 00:10:42,829
Verify again and you get valid false,
reason revoked.

64
00:10:42,829 --> 00:10:55,782
The credential isn't deleted. It stays
on-chain with its revoked flag, so there's

65
00:10:55,782 --> 00:11:04,750
a permanent record that it existed and was
withdrawn.

66
00:11:05,000 --> 00:11:15,908
Unlike expiry, revocation is one-way: a
revoked credential can't be renewed.

67
00:11:15,908 --> 00:11:31,775
If the user becomes eligible again, issue
a new credential with a new ID. That keeps

68
00:11:31,775 --> 00:11:34,750
the history honest.

69
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:48,123
The admin can also remove an issuer, so it
can't issue anything new.

70
00:11:48,123 --> 00:11:58,218
Here's the important part: credentials it
already issued stay valid.

71
00:11:58,218 --> 00:12:11,342
Removal stops future issuance; it doesn't
revoke the past. If an issuer is

72
00:12:11,342 --> 00:12:25,475
compromised, its credentials must be
revoked one by one, and the issuer has to

73
00:12:25,475 --> 00:12:34,560
do that itself, because only the issuer
can revoke.

74
00:12:34,560 --> 00:12:47,684
Verifiers that care can also check the
issuer against their own allowlist. We'll

75
00:12:47,684 --> 00:12:54,750
do exactly that in the integration video.

76
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:58,196
So, the full lifecycle.

77
00:12:58,196 --> 00:13:08,585
The admin registers issuers. An issuer
issues a credential, which may wait for

78
00:13:08,585 --> 00:13:14,179
its activation time, then verifies as
valid.

79
00:13:14,179 --> 00:13:26,964
It stops verifying when it expires, until
the issuer renews it, or for good when the

80
00:13:26,964 --> 00:13:29,362
issuer revokes it.

81
00:13:29,362 --> 00:13:38,951
Subjects share IDs, verifiers check them,
and nobody handles the underlying personal

82
00:13:38,951 --> 00:13:39,750
data.

83
00:13:40,000 --> 00:13:53,529
Every state change also emits a contract
event. CRED issued carries the credential

84
00:13:53,529 --> 00:14:08,099
ID, subject, issuer, type and expiry. CRED
revoked carries the ID and the issuer.

85
00:14:08,099 --> 00:14:19,547
Indexers and dashboards follow these
events to track issuance across the

86
00:14:19,547 --> 00:14:24,750
network without polling every credential.

87
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:30,605
That's a credential's whole life.

88
00:14:30,605 --> 00:14:45,177
In the final video we'll put it all
together and integrate Soroban Identity

89
00:14:45,177 --> 00:14:59,750
into a real app, with wallets, backend
verification and a path to mainnet.
